CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe chronic malabsorption leading to critical volume depletion and profound anemia. The combination of steatorrhea, refractory iron deficiency, aphthous ulcers, and a vesicular rash strongly suggests undiagnosed Coeliac Disease causing total villous atrophy. The chronic failure to absorb fluid and nutrients has resulted in critical orthostatic hypotension, placing the patient at high risk for a syncopal fall. Delaying assessment until standard in-hours would have left the patient severely symptomatic and at risk of traumatic injury from fainting. Urgent after-hours assessment was vital to confirm the hemodynamic instability, institute immediate fall precautions, advise strictly against initiating a gluten-free diet prior to definitive serological testing, and arrange urgent daytime comprehensive pathology. The expected investigation results include strongly positive IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase (anti-tTG) antibodies with villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia on duodenal biopsy that would assist in confirming the diagnosis at specialist review.

